hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today we're gonna go over how  to conduct a most probable number test  so a most probable number test or MPN is  sometimes because of how the method is  referred to at the NPN method this is a  method that is used to count bacteria to  measure the amount of bacteria in a  sample and it is ideal for estimating  the number of microbes and very dilute  samples so when we say dilute very  dilute samples we need samples where the  bacteria are not very concentrated so  we're not talking about lab plates or or  lab cultures where you can do a plate  count or an optical density these are  samples where there are much fewer  bacteria present or at least that's what  you suspect an example of this would be  various kinds of water and food testing  so for example testing certain kinds of  water sources for coliform bacteria  coliform bacteria are bacteria that can  ferment lactose and we have a lot of  coliform bacteria in human feces and so  if there's coliforms and water that's  typically indicative of fecal  contamination so the way the MPN method  works is it uses changes in either color  of broth or changes in termina tea  that's the cloudiness that you see when  bacteria are growing in liquid culture  so it looks for either color change or  turbidity change to detect bacterial  growth  and then you look at how many tubes  either changed color or have noted  turbidity and you can count those tubes  and then interpret your results by  comparing your results to a standard  reference table and we'll go over an  example of that in a second  but when you are using the standard  reference table it's important that you  realize that the reference table will  give you a number a most probable number  an NPN and then an NPN is equal to 1 CF  u CF U is a common way of referring to  bacteria and it stands for colony  forming unit and basically that just  means one viable cell so just to recap  this method gives you a number a most  probable number for how many bacteria  are in a certain sample and that equates  to one colony forming unit which is the  same thing as saying one viable cell so  one living cell once all that's capable  of reproducing so let's go through an  example first you have your inoculation  and so in this example I'm looking at a  water sample perhaps it's from a pond  perhaps it's from look of recreational  swimming area perhaps it's a drinking  water sample we have some water sample  here and then we have three sets of five  lactose broth tubes and remember that in  this example we're looking for coliform  bacteria and coliform bacteria can  ferment lactose you could do this method  with other kinds of tests where you're  looking for a different kind of color  change or a different growth in  turbidity and different kinds of broth  depending on the type  bacteria you're looking at so here we  have our three sets of five lactose  brought tubes and what we do is we take  ten milliliters from the drinking water  sample and inoculate each of these five  - so ten milliliters added to each one  then we take one milliliter of the water  sample and add it to each of these tubes  and then we take 0.1 milliliters of  water from sample and add it to each of  these tubes and so we're having this  tenfold decrease each time in the amount  of water that's being added and so  theoretically in the amount of viable  coliforms that are being added to these  tubes then we incubate overnight at 37  degrees typically you want to incubate  for like 24 hours for this particular  method then it's time to read results  and what that means is you're looking  for the color change so when coliform  bacteria are present in these broth  tubes they will ferment the lactose and  that results in a color change and so  for this particular test we have four  positive tubes here so four of them sort  of color change one did not we have  three positive tubes here three showed a  color change - did not and then where we  were adding the smallest amount of the  water sample we've just got one positive  tube so one changed color and four did  not and so that is our results that we  then want to come and compare in the  reference table so our pattern if you  will was four three one and so we look  at our reference table if you're a  student this is often in the back of a  textbook we're gonna or in the back of  them like a lab manual so you look for  the pattern of four three one and this  tells you that if you see the four three  one pattern the MPS  the most probable number of coliforms in  the original sample was 33 per 100 mils  now if we have gotten different patterns  up here if it had been you know four for  one or if it had been five to one  different patterns will result in a  different most probable number but with  this pattern of four three and one the  best answer statistically is that for  every 100 milliliters of that sample we  have three Cup we have 33 coliform  bacteria the table also gives us a  confidence for a 95% confidence range  and for the pattern for 3-1 the 95%  confidence range is 11 to 93 so the most  probable number is 11 to 90 per 100  milliliters and all this means is that  whereas 33 is like the best gas the most  sort of statistically supported gas you  can be 95% confident that in this water  sample that gave this pattern there is  somewhere between 11 and 93 viable  coliform bacteria per 100 mils of this  sample so that is how you conduct an NPN  test if you want to learn more about  bacterial growth you can check out my  video on bacterial growth curves and  binary fission but that's it for today  thanks for watching biology professor
 

